1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,975
The Ointment Outlasted
the Empire It Paid For

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:07,256
There is a fair chance that
somewhere in your home,

3
00:00:07,336 --> 00:00:11,143
or on a shelf at the pharmacy
nearest you, there is a small

4
00:00:11,223 --> 00:00:14,650
six-sided glass jar with a
leaping tiger on the lid.

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:18,349
Tiger Balm is sold in more
than a hundred countries

6
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:21,684
and turns up at a suburban
American drugstore

7
00:00:21,764 --> 00:00:25,025
as readily as at a
Singapore provision shop.

8
00:00:25,025 --> 00:00:28,907
Almost nobody who buys it
knows that the jar is the last

9
00:00:28,987 --> 00:00:33,010
everyday trace of a fortune
that once also ran a stable of

10
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:37,183
newspapers and a bank, and paid
for the hillside of painted

11
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:41,225
concrete demons that Singaporeans
know as Haw Par Villa.

12
00:00:41,225 --> 00:00:44,879
The story begins not in
Singapore but in Rangoon,

13
00:00:44,959 --> 00:00:48,993
where a Hakka herbalist named
Aw Chu Kin had emigrated

14
00:00:49,073 --> 00:00:53,184
from Fujian and, around 1870,
opened an apothecary shop

15
00:00:53,264 --> 00:00:57,150
called Eng Aun Tong, the
Hall of Everlasting Peace.

16
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:00,693
He had two sons who would
matter: Aw Boon Haw,

17
00:01:00,773 --> 00:01:04,789
born in 1882, whose given
name means "gentle tiger",

18
00:01:04,869 --> 00:01:08,650
and Aw Boon Par, born in
1888, "gentle leopard".

19
00:01:08,650 --> 00:01:13,170
When their father died in 1908
the brothers inherited the shop

20
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:18,141
and the family's recipes, among
them a warming ointment of camphor,

21
00:01:18,221 --> 00:01:22,450
menthol, cajuput and clove
oil in a petroleum-jelly base.

22
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:25,309
They refined that
ointment, packed it in the

23
00:01:25,389 --> 00:01:28,514
little hexagonal jars
that are still used today,

24
00:01:28,594 --> 00:01:31,800
and named it Tiger Balm
after the elder brother.

25
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,242
The division of labour between them

26
00:01:34,322 --> 00:01:36,700
held for the rest of their lives.

27
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:41,650
Boon Par was the quiet one who ran
the factory and kept the books.

28
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:45,855
Boon Haw was the salesman, and
he had an instinct for publicity

29
00:01:45,935 --> 00:01:49,675
that was decades ahead of
the market he was selling to.

30
00:01:49,675 --> 00:01:52,670
By about 1920 he was reckoned to be

31
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:56,000
among the richest Chinese in Rangoon.

32
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,109
In 1926, with an eye on the
much larger market in China,

33
00:02:00,189 --> 00:02:05,725
the brothers moved their headquarters
and their main factory to Singapore.

34
00:02:05,725 --> 00:02:09,665
The new plant on Neil Road,
in Chinatown, could turn out

35
00:02:09,745 --> 00:02:13,550
roughly ten times what the
Rangoon works had managed.

36
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:17,388
The building still stands: a
conserved corner block with

37
00:02:17,468 --> 00:02:21,447
a hexagonal pavilion on the
roof said to echo the shape of

38
00:02:21,527 --> 00:02:25,375
the balm jar, and once a
model tiger over the entrance.

39
00:02:25,375 --> 00:02:29,200
What set the business
apart was the advertising.

40
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,625
Boon Haw bought space
relentlessly in the Chinese

41
00:02:32,705 --> 00:02:36,559
and English press, wrote copy
that told little stories,

42
00:02:36,639 --> 00:02:39,500
and gave away free samples by the crate.

43
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:43,974
He commissioned "tiger cars", motor
cars and delivery vans painted

44
00:02:44,054 --> 00:02:48,527
in stripes with a snarling tiger's
head mounted over the radiator,

45
00:02:48,607 --> 00:02:52,667
red bulbs for eyes and a horn
modified to sound like a roar,

46
00:02:52,747 --> 00:02:57,025
and sent them touring from town
to town as rolling billboards.

47
00:02:57,025 --> 00:03:02,546
The leaping-tiger trademark went onto
every jar, every wall he could rent and,

48
00:03:02,626 --> 00:03:06,575
in time, the mastheads of
newspapers he owned outright.

49
00:03:06,575 --> 00:03:10,900
The ointment threw off enough
cash to build a conglomerate.

50
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:14,906
In 1929 Boon Haw founded the
Chinese-language daily Sin

51
00:03:14,986 --> 00:03:18,917
Chew Jit Poh in Singapore,
the first of some seventeen

52
00:03:18,997 --> 00:03:23,226
newspapers he would start
across South-East Asia and China

53
00:03:23,306 --> 00:03:27,311
between then and 1951,
including the Sing Tao papers in

54
00:03:27,391 --> 00:03:31,700
Hong Kong and, later, the
English-language Tiger Standard.

55
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:35,440
In 1950 he opened Chung
Khiaw Bank on Robinson

56
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,675
Road and served as its first chairman.

57
00:03:38,675 --> 00:03:41,562
There were also rubber holdings, property,

58
00:03:41,642 --> 00:03:45,175
and for a time the island
now called Coney Island.

59
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:49,450
Alongside the empire-building
was serious philanthropy.

60
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:54,138
Boon Haw gave away more than a
million dollars in his lifetime

61
00:03:54,218 --> 00:03:58,983
to schools and hospitals across
the region, and donated heavily

62
00:03:59,063 --> 00:04:03,600
to China's war relief funds
after Japan's invasion in 1937.

63
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,763
His record during the Japanese Occupation

64
00:04:06,843 --> 00:04:09,375
that followed is more contested.

65
00:04:09,375 --> 00:04:13,018
He spent the war in Hong
Kong, met Japanese officials,

66
00:04:13,098 --> 00:04:17,569
and afterwards faced accusations
of collaboration and opportunism;

67
00:04:17,649 --> 00:04:20,947
defenders argued he was
a marked man as a wealthy

68
00:04:21,027 --> 00:04:24,739
overseas Chinese who had
bankrolled China's resistance,

69
00:04:24,819 --> 00:04:29,025
and did what was needed to
protect his people and his assets.

70
00:04:29,025 --> 00:04:32,525
Both readings have been argued ever since.

71
00:04:32,525 --> 00:04:36,151
In 1935 the brothers
bought a seafront hill on

72
00:04:36,231 --> 00:04:39,856
Pasir Panjang Road, on
the rural south-western

73
00:04:39,936 --> 00:04:43,400
coast well away from
the Chinatown factory.

74
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:47,811
There the architect Ho Kwong
Yew built an Art Deco villa,

75
00:04:47,891 --> 00:04:52,775
finished in 1937, which Boon Haw
gave to his homesick brother.

76
00:04:52,775 --> 00:04:57,352
Around it he laid out a garden,
open to the public without charge,

77
00:04:57,432 --> 00:05:01,091
filled with painted statues
and tableaux illustrating

78
00:05:01,171 --> 00:05:05,475
Chinese folklore and Confucian,
Buddhist and Taoist morality.

79
00:05:05,475 --> 00:05:09,062
The set piece was, and remains,
the Ten Courts of Hell,

80
00:05:09,142 --> 00:05:13,075
a walk-through of the punishments
said to await the wicked.

81
00:05:13,075 --> 00:05:16,164
Similar gardens went up in Hong Kong and

82
00:05:16,244 --> 00:05:19,650
in the family's ancestral
county in Fujian.

83
00:05:19,650 --> 00:05:22,300
The war reached the hill too.

84
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:26,012
Japanese forces used the
high ground as a lookout,

85
00:05:26,092 --> 00:05:29,576
the villa was bombed,
and after 1945 the ruined

86
00:05:29,656 --> 00:05:33,675
mansion was pulled down,
though the statues survived.

87
00:05:33,675 --> 00:05:37,401
Boon Par had already gone back to Rangoon,

88
00:05:37,481 --> 00:05:39,475
where he died in 1944.

89
00:05:39,475 --> 00:05:43,592
Boon Haw kept adding to the
garden until his own death,

90
00:05:43,672 --> 00:05:48,094
of heart failure, in Honolulu
on the 4th of September 1954,

91
00:05:48,174 --> 00:05:51,150
on his way back from the United States.

92
00:05:51,150 --> 00:05:53,200
He was 72.

93
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:58,475
What happened next is a study in
how a family fortune comes apart.

94
00:05:58,475 --> 00:06:03,869
In 1969 the family bundled the
businesses into a listed company,

95
00:06:03,949 --> 00:06:06,600
Haw Par Brothers International.

96
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:10,434
Within two years they had
sold control to Slater Walker,

97
00:06:10,514 --> 00:06:16,096
the British investment group run by Jim
Slater, and Boon Par's son Aw Cheng Chye,

98
00:06:16,176 --> 00:06:20,650
who had been chairman, died
suddenly on a trip to South America.

99
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:25,253
Slater Walker broke the conglomerate
up: the Chung Khiaw Bank

100
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:28,555
stake went to United
Overseas Bank in 1972,

101
00:06:28,635 --> 00:06:34,467
the newspapers were sold off, and Tiger
Balm production moved out of the Neil

102
00:06:34,547 --> 00:06:39,000
Road building in 1971 to a
contractor's factory in Jurong.

103
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,197
In 1975 Singapore authorities
alleged that Slater Walker

104
00:06:43,277 --> 00:06:48,010
executives had rigged Haw Par's
shares and stripped its assets;

105
00:06:48,090 --> 00:06:51,523
Singapore sought Jim
Slater's extradition from

106
00:06:51,603 --> 00:06:55,575
Britain and was refused,
and he resigned soon after.

107
00:06:55,575 --> 00:06:59,977
From 1978 the banker Wee Cho
Yaw of UOB became chairman,

108
00:07:00,057 --> 00:07:04,700
and his family has been the
controlling shareholder since.

109
00:07:04,700 --> 00:07:08,725
The company is now called
Haw Par Corporation.

110
00:07:08,725 --> 00:07:12,000
The other pieces went their own ways.

111
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:16,821
Sin Chew Jit Poh in Singapore
printed its last issue on the 15th

112
00:07:16,901 --> 00:07:21,185
of March 1983 and merged with
a rival into Lianhe Zaobao,

113
00:07:21,265 --> 00:07:26,625
whose masthead still carries the
words "Sin Chew" to mark the descent.

114
00:07:26,625 --> 00:07:29,895
The Neil Road factory building was

115
00:07:29,975 --> 00:07:33,325
given conservation status in 1992.

116
00:07:33,325 --> 00:07:38,397
Haw Par Villa, which the family had
run as a free attraction for decades,

117
00:07:38,477 --> 00:07:41,291
was taken over by the state in the 1980s;

118
00:07:41,371 --> 00:07:45,525
from around 1990 it was relaunched
as a ticketed theme park,

119
00:07:45,605 --> 00:07:49,901
"Dragon World", with animatronics
and an "oriental Disneyland"

120
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:54,206
pitch that reportedly lost about
31 million Singapore dollars

121
00:07:54,286 --> 00:07:58,450
over a decade before admission
was made free again in 1998.

122
00:07:58,450 --> 00:08:03,875
Haw Par Corporation today is a
quiet company listed in Singapore.

123
00:08:03,875 --> 00:08:07,624
Tiger Balm and its sister
medicated oils are almost the

124
00:08:07,704 --> 00:08:11,315
whole of its revenue, and
the balm sells in more than

125
00:08:11,395 --> 00:08:15,423
a hundred countries, made in
Singapore, Malaysia and China,

126
00:08:15,503 --> 00:08:18,775
with roughly a third of
sales now outside Asia.

127
00:08:18,775 --> 00:08:23,325
But most of the company's profit
does not come from balm at all.

128
00:08:23,325 --> 00:08:27,204
Decades ago the group acquired
large blocks of shares in

129
00:08:27,284 --> 00:08:31,164
UOB and the property developer
UOL, and never sold them;

130
00:08:31,244 --> 00:08:34,840
those holdings are worth
several billion dollars and

131
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:38,950
the dividends alone now exceed
what Tiger Balm brings in.

132
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:42,319
The Tiger Balm company,
in effect, is a well-run

133
00:08:42,399 --> 00:08:46,350
ointment brand wrapped around
an old bank shareholding.

134
00:08:46,350 --> 00:08:49,863
Where it fits in the bigger
story: one of the largest

135
00:08:49,943 --> 00:08:53,252
overseas-Chinese fortunes
of the twentieth century

136
00:08:53,332 --> 00:08:56,574
was built and run from
Singapore, then scattered,

137
00:08:56,654 --> 00:09:00,450
and the family name has largely
faded from local memory.

138
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:03,536
Three fragments of it
are still in plain sight:

139
00:09:03,616 --> 00:09:08,050
a jar in the bathroom cabinet, the
words on a newspaper's masthead,

140
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:10,880
and a hillside of concrete demons at Pasir

141
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:14,800
Panjang that the government
now keeps as a heritage park.
